Obstructive Uropathy & Chronic Urinary Retention (Secondary to Neglected BPH)
Genitourinary Renal & Mens Health
Painless massive distended bladder, Continuous overflow incontinence, Bilateral hydronephrosis on ultrasound

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GRM-029
Reason for Visit: Progressive, profound lethargy, severe nausea, and a massive lower abdominal mass combined with continuous urinary leakage.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and adult child
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage revealed a patient with a massive, painless abdominal mass, continuous involuntary urinary dribbling, and new-onset systemic symptoms of uraemia including severe nausea and fatigue. Urgent after-hours assessment was critically necessary to rule out advanced intra-abdominal malignancy, diagnose massive chronic urinary retention, and execute immediate, controlled bladder decompression to prevent irreversible acute-on-chronic renal failure and potentially fatal electrolyte derangements.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I am constantly leaking urine, my stomach is swollen like a balloon, and I am too nauseous to eat anything at all.
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a long-standing history of worsening, progressive lower urinary tract symptoms, including severe hesitancy and a poor stream, which they have neglected for over two years. They describe the insidious development of a massive, painless distended bladder. They suffer from continuous involuntary urinary dribbling, indicating severe overflow incontinence. Over the past week, they have developed progressive bilateral leg oedema. Most concerningly, over the last 48 hours, they have exhibited classic uraemic symptoms, including profound nausea, severe anorexia, and generalized, debilitating fatigue. They completely deny any acute severe suprapubic pain, despite the massive abdominal distension.
Functional Status: Bedbound currently due to severe uraemic fatigue and nausea; previously independent.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of falls due to weakness; profound neglect of chronic medical symptoms.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 165/95 | HR: 90 | Temp: 36.5C | RR: 18 | SpO2: 97% | Wt: 86 Kg
General: Chronically ill-appearing, pale, uraemic fetor detectable on the breath, exhibiting extreme lethargy and visible fluid overload.
Physical Exam: Abdomen: A massive, painless, firm, and dull-to-percussion midline lower abdominal mass extends three finger-breadths above the umbilicus. Bilaterally enlarged kidneys are palpable, indicating severe gross hydronephrosis. Genitourinary: Continuous overflow incontinence is observed. Digital rectal examination reveals a markedly enlarged, firm, and smooth prostate without focal nodules. Extremities: 2 plus pitting oedema bilaterally. Expected investigation results will reveal a massive post-void residual volume exceeding 1500 mL on ultrasound, bilateral severe hydroureteronephrosis, and acutely elevated serum creatinine.
Environmental Observations: House smells heavily of stale urine; multiple soiled towels used as makeshift incontinence pads.
Medication Reconciliation: No medications taken. Patient has entirely avoided medical care for years out of fear.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Obstructive Uropathy & Chronic Urinary Retention (Secondary to Neglected BPH) - Status: Exacerbated (Uraemic Crisis)
Differentials:

Intra-abdominal Malignancy/Tumour - Ruled out/unlikely as the massive abdominal mass completely resolved following urethral catheterization, confirming it was an overdistended bladder.

Acute Urinary Retention - Ruled out/unlikely due to the complete absence of severe, agonizing pain; the bladder has slowly stretched over months, destroying the sensory nerves.

Liver Failure with Ascites - Ruled out/unlikely due to the mass being centrally located and dull (bladder), rather than causing shifting dullness, and the specific presence of overflow incontinence.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents in a state of post-renal acute kidney injury and uraemia, caused by massive, chronic, painless urinary retention secondary to neglected benign prostatic hyperplasia. The bladder has stretched silently over months, transmitting severe back-pressure to the kidneys and causing bilateral hydronephrosis. The resultant uraemia is causing the systemic nausea and lethargy. An urgent after-hours intervention was absolutely mandatory to definitively diagnose the mass, physically insert a catheter to drain the obstruction, and arrange immediate laboratory assessment. Failing to decompress this system immediately would lead directly to irreversible end-stage renal failure or fatal hyperkalaemia.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Inserted a 16 French indwelling urethral catheter. Drained 1800 mL of clear, dilute urine. Catheter clamped after the initial 1000 mL and drained slowly over the next hour to prevent haemorrhagia ex vacuo and severe vasovagal collapse. Arranged urgent morning domiciliary blood collection (UECs, CMP) to evaluate renal function and potassium levels.
Positive Behavioral Change: To manage the expected massive post-obstructive diuresis safely, the patient is instructed to drink exactly one glass of water for every time the catheter bag requires emptying. By physically linking fluid intake directly to measured fluid output, the patient proactively prevents severe dehydration, reinforcing the critical habit of strict fluid balance during the recovery phase.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Domiciliary phlebotomy and community nursing referral generated for daily catheter care and strict fluid balance monitoring.
DME/Safety (Optional): Leg bag and high-capacity overnight drainage bag provided.
Follow-up: GP review tomorrow afternoon to review the urgent renal function bloods; high probability of requiring hospital admission for IV fluid replacement if diuresis is profound.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ambulance transfer to the Emergency Department is mandated if the patient develops profound dizziness when sitting up, confusion, severe muscle cramps, or if the catheter stops draining entirely.

PATIENT INFORMATION

